    "result_text": "Done. Tests are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## What I changed\n\nI agreed with the finding \u2014 the ~20-line Luhn algorithm inlined in the MapStruct interface broke the mapper's clear two-tier convention (trivial formatting inline; algorithmic derivations delegated to a `model`-package helper, as with `OwnerIdentity.of` and `CityRegions.localityOf`).\n\n- **New `model/LuhnCheckDigit.java`** \u2014 a focused domain helper (`LuhnCheckDigit.of(String)`) holding the algorithm, with class-level javadoc explaining why it lives beside `Owner`, matching the style of its siblings. Returns `null` for a null input.\n- **`OwnerMapper.toCheckDigit`** reduced to a one-line delegation: `return LuhnCheckDigit.of(owner.getCustomerCode());`, keeping the existing javadoc and matching `toIdentityKey`/`toLocality`.\n\nOn the reviewer's two extraction options, I took the `LuhnCheckDigit.of(String)` utility rather than introducing a full `CustomerCode` domain type and moving the format logic out of `AssignCustomerCode`. The check digit is a general-purpose numeric algorithm that's conceptually independent of the customer-code *format*, so a dedicated Luhn helper is the more cohesive home; folding a full `CustomerCode` refactor (relocating the `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` format rules) into this change would be broader restructuring than the finding requires and would touch the create pipeline. This satisfies the core asks \u2014 algorithm out of the mapper, independently unit-testable, mapper thin and consistent \u2014 while keeping the change scoped.\n\nThe acceptance behaviour is unchanged (the algorithm is byte-for-byte identical), and the full suite passes.",
