        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe check-digit computation is correct in intent but placed in the wrong unit, breaking this mapper's established convention.\n\n**The convention in `OwnerMapper` is a two-tier split, and this change violates it:**\n\n- **Trivial formatting derivations stay inline** as one-liner `default` methods: `toDisplayName`, `toInitials`, `toContactPreference` (all \u22643 lines of straight formatting).\n- **Non-trivial / algorithmic derivations delegate to a dedicated domain helper** in the `model` package, with the mapper method a thin one-line wrapper:\n  - `toIdentityKey(owner)` \u2192 `OwnerIdentity.of(owner)`\n  - `toLocality(owner)` \u2192 `CityRegions.localityOf(...)`\n\n  These helpers even carry class-level javadoc explaining *why* they live beside `Owner` (`OwnerTelephones`, `CityRegions`, `OwnerIdentity`).\n\n`toCheckDigit` is a ~20-line general-purpose numeric algorithm (Luhn) \u2014 the single heaviest method in the interface, heavier than `OwnerIdentity.of` itself \u2014 yet it is embedded directly in the MapStruct interface. A maintainer here would expect it to follow `toIdentityKey`/`toLocality`, not to be the one algorithm inlined into the mapper. As written, the mapper interface is starting to become a catch-all for logic that the codebase otherwise keeps in `model`.\n\n**Concrete change:**\n\n- Extract the Luhn logic into a domain helper in `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model` \u2014 e.g. a `LuhnCheckDigit.of(String)` utility, or (given the digit is derived *from the customerCode*) a method on a `CustomerCode` domain type that would sit naturally beside the format logic already living in `AssignCustomerCode` (`<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`). The `customerCode` concept currently has no single home; this is a chance to give it one rather than scatter more of its rules.\n- Reduce `toCheckDigit` to a one-line delegation matching its siblings, e.g. `return LuhnCheckDigit.of(owner.getCustomerCode());` (helper returns `null` for a null code), keeping the existing method javadoc.\n\nThis keeps the algorithm unit-testable on its own, keeps the mapper thin and consistent, and matches how every other non-trivial derived field in this exact interface is already handled."
      }
    ]
